One dusk, a falcon with eyes of storm perched on her doorway. It spoke in a language of wind, telling Lira of a village beyond the mountains, where people wore dull colors like shackles and forgot how to laugh. The falcon pressed a single seed into her palm—a seed shaped like a teardrop, warm as a newborn sun. “Plant this where your heart aches,” the bird rasped, and vanished.

